1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:02,520
In this video, we're
going to be talking

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:05,415
about building kernel modules.

3
00:00:05,415 --> 00:00:09,240
We're going to cover a kernel
module layout and content.

4
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:13,005
The end, how to construct a
really simple kernel module.

5
00:00:13,005 --> 00:00:14,700
We'll talk about kernel

6
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,410
module development and
how that compares with

7
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:18,780
the application and system
level programming that we've

8
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:22,440
done with the first
part of this course.

9
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:26,325
One of the first examples
that you'll see in the book,

10
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:28,020
in the Linux Device
Drivers book,

11
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:29,715
is the hello world driver.

12
00:00:29,715 --> 00:00:31,830
The classic introduction to

13
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:34,410
whatever programming
language you're using.

14
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:36,240
We'll have a hello world driver

15
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,085
that we do as our first step.

16
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:40,290
When you're looking at the
examples from the book,

17
00:00:40,290 --> 00:00:43,040
I wanted to point out
there's some comments

18
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,000
about an FTP site where
you can find source code.

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,855
Please instead of using that,

20
00:00:47,855 --> 00:00:49,910
use the repository
that's linked here.

21
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:53,950
This one is kept up to date and

22
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:56,160
will work with the kernel

23
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,490
that's used in your
Ubuntu virtual machines.

24
00:00:58,490 --> 00:01:02,510
You can try this out on your
Ubuntu virtual machine.

25
00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:04,790
There's also some content
in the book about

26
00:01:04,790 --> 00:01:08,215
downloading a kernel from
kernel.org and compiling it.

27
00:01:08,215 --> 00:01:09,810
You can certainly do that.

28
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:12,950
But this repository,

29
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:15,320
it should work with the
kernel that's a part of

30
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:19,225
your Ubuntu Linux
virtual machine.

31
00:01:19,225 --> 00:01:20,850
If you just go into

32
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:23,390
the misc-modules directory
and run make and then do

33
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:28,215
insmod as root with
the hello.kao file.

34
00:01:28,215 --> 00:01:30,140
You'll see in /var/log/syslog,

35
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,580
you'll see a message like this,

36
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:33,425
saying Hello, World.

37
00:01:33,425 --> 00:01:35,610
Goodbye, cruel world.

38
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:39,580
The goodbye, will come out
during the rmmod stuff,

39
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:41,210
but you'll be able to
see that your driver was

40
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:43,720
loaded and unloaded
in the kernel.

41
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:45,920
One of the other messages
you noticed there

42
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,960
was the tainted kernel message.

43
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:50,389
It says, loading
out-of-tree module

44
00:01:50,389 --> 00:01:53,005
taints kernel. What
does this mean?

45
00:01:53,005 --> 00:01:55,505
It means that you've

46
00:01:55,505 --> 00:01:58,460
changed the content of a
kernel while it's running,

47
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:00,170
and you've loaded a
module that wasn't

48
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:02,180
a part of the standard
kernel build.

49
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:04,085
This is just warning you that

50
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:05,990
if you have problems
with your system,

51
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:08,585
it's likely that
it could be from

52
00:02:08,585 --> 00:02:10,130
the module that you just loaded

53
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:13,190
that kernel developers
don't know anything about.

54
00:02:13,190 --> 00:02:15,770
It's also a signal

55
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:17,765
that would show up in
the logs that would tell

56
00:02:17,765 --> 00:02:21,230
a kernel developer that maybe
the reason that your system

57
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:24,410
crashed might not be due
to the code in the kernel,

58
00:02:24,410 --> 00:02:27,450
it might be due to this
module that you added.

59
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:33,135
The simple hello.c example,

60
00:02:33,135 --> 00:02:37,040
you can see how

61
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:39,740
this is used in the file
that's linked here.

62
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:41,900
It's really just a

63
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:43,880
couple parts that
we'll talk about here.

64
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:45,500
One is specifying the license of

65
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,620
the module and then specifying

66
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:49,910
the two different functions

67
00:02:49,910 --> 00:02:51,530
that are associated
with loading and

68
00:02:51,530 --> 00:02:52,940
unloading the module with

69
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:55,405
this module_init and
module_exit macro.

70
00:02:55,405 --> 00:02:57,530
One other thing to
highlight here is

71
00:02:57,530 --> 00:02:59,420
that we're using printk and not

72
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:01,640
printf for the interactions

73
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,420
in the prints that
go to the syslog.

74
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,565
The module_init with
a function name

75
00:03:07,565 --> 00:03:08,660
is really just a macro that

76
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:10,640
identifies the function
that's called whenever the

77
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:13,030
module's loaded from insmod.

78
00:03:13,030 --> 00:03:15,990
It's similar to int main,

79
00:03:15,990 --> 00:03:18,130
void, that you'd use
for system programming.

80
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:22,115
But it's different in that
it's not going to stay running

81
00:03:22,115 --> 00:03:26,050
the way that int
main, void, would.

82
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:29,120
It's going to expect
you to load something,

83
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:30,830
get your kernel module setup and

84
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:33,470
registered with the
kernel, and then exit.

85
00:03:33,470 --> 00:03:36,710
It's similar to the way

86
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:38,300
an event-driven
application would work,

87
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:40,070
where you're going
to set something up

88
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:42,035
and then you will wait
for events to happen.

89
00:03:42,035 --> 00:03:44,240
Those events are going to be
coming from the kernel based

90
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:45,350
on the initialization that you

91
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:47,260
set up in your module in it.

92
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:50,870
The module_exit macro
is going to identify

93
00:03:50,870 --> 00:03:52,100
the function that's called when

94
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:54,370
module is unloaded from rmmod.

95
00:03:54,370 --> 00:03:57,140
That one is similar to

96
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:00,125
the signal handler that
you'd get from SIGTERM.

97
00:04:00,125 --> 00:04:02,060
But the difference is that
it's actually going to

98
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:05,020
completely exit your module.

99
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,520
Another thing to
note about kernel

100
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:10,130
programming versus system
programming, if you remember,

101
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:13,475
we talked about how if
you forget to free memory

102
00:04:13,475 --> 00:04:17,240
in application programming
or system programming,

103
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,160
the kernel can track
that for you and

104
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,885
can know that when
the process exits,

105
00:04:20,885 --> 00:04:21,950
all the memory that's

106
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:23,810
associated with the
process can be freed.

107
00:04:23,810 --> 00:04:26,900
But now that we're
working in kernel space,

108
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:28,860
we don't have those
protections anymore,

109
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:30,300
and so if you

110
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:33,620
forgot to free some memory
that your driver had

111
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:36,410
allocated and you don't
release memory or

112
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:39,860
other resources with your
module_exit function,

113
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,750
then those are just
going to be lost and

114
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:44,930
that's a permanent memory
leak until reboot.

115
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:47,120
The stakes become a
little bit higher as

116
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,675
we start working
in kernel space.

117
00:04:49,675 --> 00:04:52,590
Why would we use
printk and not printf?

118
00:04:52,590 --> 00:04:54,210
The answer is we
don't have libc,

119
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:56,690
so any of those functions
that you're used to using,

120
00:04:56,690 --> 00:04:58,475
and you've probably used for

121
00:04:58,475 --> 00:04:59,960
any application
development you've

122
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,645
done in Linux in the past,

123
00:05:01,645 --> 00:05:03,785
most of those aren't
going to be there.

124
00:05:03,785 --> 00:05:06,020
For instance, standard dio.h,

125
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:07,730
the common header
files that you'd

126
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:09,890
use that are based on libc,

127
00:05:09,890 --> 00:05:12,560
those are not going to be
available to us anymore.

128
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:13,835
You're going to have to use

129
00:05:13,835 --> 00:05:16,280
the utility functions that are

130
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:17,780
provided by the kernel instead

131
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:20,130
and printk is one of those.

132
00:05:20,130 --> 00:05:22,360
The module license
is just going to

133
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:24,160
clarify that in this case,

134
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:26,275
the module bears a free license,

135
00:05:26,275 --> 00:05:29,140
so one that's
compatible with GPL.

136
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:32,290
The reason for doing this is
that I talked about before,

137
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:34,900
the licensing considerations
in whether or not a module

138
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:39,025
can be licensed proprietary.

139
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:41,710
That was left
deliberately ambiguous.

140
00:05:41,710 --> 00:05:43,540
However, there are
some calls into

141
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:45,640
the kernel where developers
have specifically said,

142
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,170
in order to use this call,

143
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:51,850
your driver definitely needs
to have a GPL license.

144
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:54,985
For those calls,

145
00:05:54,985 --> 00:05:56,170
what they're looking
for you to do

146
00:05:56,170 --> 00:05:57,550
is specifically state in

147
00:05:57,550 --> 00:05:59,755
your module source code

148
00:05:59,755 --> 00:06:02,770
that your source
is GPL compliant.

149
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:07,300
Another thing to
note about module

150
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:12,295
versus application is
with the application,

151
00:06:12,295 --> 00:06:14,680
the kernel will help clean

152
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:16,750
up from something
like a segfault,

153
00:06:16,750 --> 00:06:18,550
take a core file for us,

154
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:22,165
and provide some
debugging information,

155
00:06:22,165 --> 00:06:24,490
but it probably won't bring
the entire system down.

156
00:06:24,490 --> 00:06:26,050
That changes now
that we're working

157
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:27,550
with kernel modules as well,

158
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:30,655
we don't have that
clean-up available for us.

159
00:06:30,655 --> 00:06:33,850
A lot of times, if you write
a problem with your driver

160
00:06:33,850 --> 00:06:35,455
that turns into a segfault

161
00:06:35,455 --> 00:06:37,630
that may crash the
entire system.

162
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:39,730
Again, the stakes
are a little bit

163
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:42,650
higher now that we're
working in kernel space.

164
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:45,610
Another thing to know about

165
00:06:45,610 --> 00:06:48,790
developing a module
versus an application,

166
00:06:48,790 --> 00:06:50,740
the kernel stack is really small

167
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:53,140
compared to applications so

168
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:55,390
the default stack
you're probably working

169
00:06:55,390 --> 00:06:58,570
with an application is around
two megabytes in size.

170
00:06:58,570 --> 00:07:02,680
You can throw huge variables in

171
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:07,750
your local variables for
your function and you

172
00:07:07,750 --> 00:07:08,980
probably won't
notice you can have

173
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:10,810
really deep call
stacks and you won't

174
00:07:10,810 --> 00:07:13,195
notice with that
large of stack size.

175
00:07:13,195 --> 00:07:14,950
However, depending on
your architecture,

176
00:07:14,950 --> 00:07:16,720
the kernel stack size
may be really small,

177
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:21,160
maybe as small as 4,000
bytes. What does that mean?

178
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:23,575
It means that all
active functions,

179
00:07:23,575 --> 00:07:24,985
including yours,

180
00:07:24,985 --> 00:07:26,590
the one that you're
writing for your driver,

181
00:07:26,590 --> 00:07:28,000
plus anything that called within

182
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,205
the kernel plus anything
you're going to call,

183
00:07:30,205 --> 00:07:31,600
all of those functions
are going to share

184
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:34,135
that probably
relatively small space.

185
00:07:34,135 --> 00:07:36,549
You really need to be
careful about large

186
00:07:36,549 --> 00:07:40,120
allocated automatic
stack variables,

187
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:41,290
you don't want to define

188
00:07:41,290 --> 00:07:45,325
a 1k buffer as a local
variable because

189
00:07:45,325 --> 00:07:47,770
there's a likelihood
that if you use it in

190
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:50,994
some specific scenario
or on some architecture,

191
00:07:50,994 --> 00:07:53,095
you probably would
run out of stack.

192
00:07:53,095 --> 00:07:55,360
Instead, the normal method for

193
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:58,210
doing memory allocation in

194
00:07:58,210 --> 00:08:01,300
Linux driver development for

195
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:05,245
any large variables would
be to allocate dynamically.

196
00:08:05,245 --> 00:08:07,270
Another important
thing to note is that

197
00:08:07,270 --> 00:08:09,835
floating-point is
generally not supported.

198
00:08:09,835 --> 00:08:11,980
There are some exceptions
but for the most part,

199
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:16,040
you won't be using float or
double or any of those types.

200
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:19,585
This drawing from a book shows

201
00:08:19,585 --> 00:08:21,970
a little bit about how
a module is actually

202
00:08:21,970 --> 00:08:24,280
linked into the kernel and
the interaction that happens

203
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:27,160
between the kernel and a module.

204
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:29,365
When we do the insmod,

205
00:08:29,365 --> 00:08:32,560
the module entry macro

206
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:35,230
has set up that init
function for us,

207
00:08:35,230 --> 00:08:37,270
set up a pointer to
the init function so

208
00:08:37,270 --> 00:08:39,310
that insmod interaction with

209
00:08:39,310 --> 00:08:41,590
a kernel can tell the kernel how

210
00:08:41,590 --> 00:08:44,575
to instantiate your module.

211
00:08:44,575 --> 00:08:46,375
So in this case,

212
00:08:46,375 --> 00:08:47,800
we're looking at a
driver that would

213
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:50,200
have call to a
function that would

214
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:51,430
do some setup for

215
00:08:51,430 --> 00:08:54,145
how future interaction with
the kernel is going to work.

216
00:08:54,145 --> 00:08:55,855
You can see, there's a call to

217
00:08:55,855 --> 00:08:58,000
a block init_queue and there's

218
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,300
a add_disk function that's

219
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:03,205
being called from
our init module.

220
00:09:03,205 --> 00:09:05,620
What these are doing
is setting up data

221
00:09:05,620 --> 00:09:10,630
that's likely dynamically
allocated by the driver,

222
00:09:10,630 --> 00:09:14,660
and then providing
that data into

223
00:09:14,670 --> 00:09:17,995
kernel structures
that will know how to

224
00:09:17,995 --> 00:09:20,995
later invoke your driver
when some action occurs.

225
00:09:20,995 --> 00:09:24,325
Using this as an example
for a block device driver,

226
00:09:24,325 --> 00:09:28,705
this would be when an
interaction is happening from

227
00:09:28,705 --> 00:09:30,610
the kernel block
layer and you need to

228
00:09:30,610 --> 00:09:33,250
actually read or write
blocks from your device.

229
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:36,340
In the block driver case,

230
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:37,915
there's a block_device ops,

231
00:09:37,915 --> 00:09:40,630
that's really just a
collection of pointers that

232
00:09:40,630 --> 00:09:42,040
points to information about

233
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:44,320
how to interact
with your module.

234
00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:48,190
When a request happens,

235
00:09:48,190 --> 00:09:49,900
there's an interaction
that happens in

236
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:51,940
the kernel that
knows how to find

237
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:55,120
the function pointers and

238
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:57,355
the data that
describes your module,

239
00:09:57,355 --> 00:09:59,560
and then be able to make
these calls back into

240
00:09:59,560 --> 00:10:02,180
your module to handle
specific events.

241
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:04,680
Once we do the insmod and
we set all of this up,

242
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:07,770
we're basically event-driven
from the kernel.

243
00:10:07,770 --> 00:10:09,030
Whenever some event happens

244
00:10:09,030 --> 00:10:10,665
that needs to interact
with our driver,

245
00:10:10,665 --> 00:10:12,900
we'll be called with
those functions that we

246
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:16,225
registered during
our insmod step.

247
00:10:16,225 --> 00:10:19,760
When it's time to
unload the module,

248
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:22,400
when there's no more
work that we need to do.

249
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:23,960
The cleanup function
is going to have

250
00:10:23,960 --> 00:10:25,460
the link to the module exit.

251
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:27,665
What we need to make
sure of is that we

252
00:10:27,665 --> 00:10:30,455
undo everything that we
did in the init function.

253
00:10:30,455 --> 00:10:32,750
We remove all those
pointers that we set up,

254
00:10:32,750 --> 00:10:34,190
so that the kernel
doesn't try to

255
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:36,170
call our driver
when it's unloaded.

256
00:10:36,170 --> 00:10:38,210
We do that in the opposite order

257
00:10:38,210 --> 00:10:40,055
of the way that we registered.

258
00:10:40,055 --> 00:10:43,085
If we started by
blk_init_queue and add_disk,

259
00:10:43,085 --> 00:10:46,460
we would delete the disk and

260
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:51,090
clean up the queue in
the rmmod function.

261
00:10:51,430 --> 00:10:54,950
If you remember, we talked
during application programming

262
00:10:54,950 --> 00:10:57,620
about the different ways
you get into kernel space.

263
00:10:57,620 --> 00:10:59,120
The one that we focused on for

264
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:01,460
the first part of the course
is through system call.

265
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:03,740
There's a system call handler
and maybe that's going to

266
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:05,930
be interacting with your
device driver in some way,

267
00:11:05,930 --> 00:11:07,775
this would be, for instance,

268
00:11:07,775 --> 00:11:12,755
an operation with
your device driver.

269
00:11:12,755 --> 00:11:14,660
Then the second way this could

270
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:16,520
happen is an
interrupt that occurs

271
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:22,400
from inside kernel space.

272
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:25,340
Then user space can
come back and pull for

273
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:29,100
some interaction later
with your driver.

274
00:11:29,230 --> 00:11:32,360
In terms of user
versus kernel space,

275
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:34,490
we've already this briefly

276
00:11:34,490 --> 00:11:35,690
in the first part of the course,

277
00:11:35,690 --> 00:11:38,450
but modern processes are
going to support enforcing

278
00:11:38,450 --> 00:11:41,450
protection against unauthorized
access to resources.

279
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:42,995
What does that mean?
It means that you have

280
00:11:42,995 --> 00:11:45,185
a special privilege level
that's associated with

281
00:11:45,185 --> 00:11:49,475
running kernel code
versus running user mode.

282
00:11:49,475 --> 00:11:52,130
They do this via
hardware support

283
00:11:52,130 --> 00:11:55,085
for different operating
modalities or levels.

284
00:11:55,085 --> 00:11:56,840
Some operating levels aren't

285
00:11:56,840 --> 00:11:58,790
able to access certain
regions of memory,

286
00:11:58,790 --> 00:12:00,530
for instance, and others are.

287
00:12:00,530 --> 00:12:01,550
You wanna make sure
that when you're

288
00:12:01,550 --> 00:12:02,870
running user space code,

289
00:12:02,870 --> 00:12:05,480
you're in the mode
that's not able to

290
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:08,555
access the all areas of memory.

291
00:12:08,555 --> 00:12:10,250
When you're running
in kernel mode,

292
00:12:10,250 --> 00:12:12,710
you probably need access
to at least more memory,

293
00:12:12,710 --> 00:12:16,055
if not all areas of memory.

294
00:12:16,055 --> 00:12:20,480
This will be a separate
address space that you can

295
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:23,210
partition both program and

296
00:12:23,210 --> 00:12:27,020
data memory into in terms
of who has access to what.

297
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:29,510
The applications that
we've talked about in

298
00:12:29,510 --> 00:12:30,560
the first part of
the semester are

299
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:31,745
going to use user space,

300
00:12:31,745 --> 00:12:34,280
that is the execution
here, there,

301
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:34,910
it's going to happen at

302
00:12:34,910 --> 00:12:37,145
the lowest privilege
operating level.

303
00:12:37,145 --> 00:12:38,810
You're going to be
limited on what hardware

304
00:12:38,810 --> 00:12:40,040
you can access and
you're going to be

305
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:44,615
limited in how you're
able to access memory.

306
00:12:44,615 --> 00:12:48,320
Then drivers will
use kernel space,

307
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:49,190
and that's going to be

308
00:12:49,190 --> 00:12:51,185
the highest privileged
operating level

309
00:12:51,185 --> 00:12:54,275
or supervisor mode
often as referred to.

310
00:12:54,275 --> 00:12:57,320
In this case, you'll enter with

311
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:58,400
the system caller hardware

312
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:00,095
interrupt like we
talked about before,

313
00:13:00,095 --> 00:13:02,285
and you'll remove those
restrictions that you have

314
00:13:02,285 --> 00:13:05,610
on access to memory,
for instance.

315
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:11,120
When you're writing the code
for your kernel driver,

316
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:13,370
in comparison to what
you'd be doing in

317
00:13:13,370 --> 00:13:15,515
user space over
system programming.

318
00:13:15,515 --> 00:13:16,730
You need to keep in mind

319
00:13:16,730 --> 00:13:17,960
that the kernel is
going to be running

320
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:20,150
multiple processes and
there might be a bunch of

321
00:13:20,150 --> 00:13:21,350
different things that
are trying to use

322
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:22,640
your driver at once.

323
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:24,200
The interrupts that
we mentioned before

324
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:25,580
will all happen asynchronously,

325
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:27,230
could happen anytime
with respect to

326
00:13:27,230 --> 00:13:29,780
the code that you're
driver's executing.

327
00:13:29,780 --> 00:13:32,930
Simultaneous multiprocessor
systems are supported,

328
00:13:32,930 --> 00:13:36,590
so it's very possible
that the same code in

329
00:13:36,590 --> 00:13:38,270
your module could be running at

330
00:13:38,270 --> 00:13:40,700
the same time on two
different processors.

331
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,980
You need to be cognizant
of that when you're

332
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:45,500
writing your driver code.
What does that mean?

333
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:47,465
It means that all kernel code,

334
00:13:47,465 --> 00:13:49,265
including the driver code
that you're writing,

335
00:13:49,265 --> 00:13:50,660
needs to be reentrant,

336
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:52,310
that you need to have

337
00:13:52,310 --> 00:13:54,290
data structures that can

338
00:13:54,290 --> 00:13:56,300
keep threads of
execution separate.

339
00:13:56,300 --> 00:13:57,860
What does that mean? It means

340
00:13:57,860 --> 00:14:00,410
the same locking that
we've talked about,

341
00:14:00,410 --> 00:14:02,300
or similar types of
locking that we've

342
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,325
talked about from the
first part of the course.

343
00:14:04,325 --> 00:14:07,175
We're going to need to be
careful about using that for

344
00:14:07,175 --> 00:14:08,270
pretty much everything we do

345
00:14:08,270 --> 00:14:11,460
with device driver development.

346
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:15,035
It's important to
note that reentrancy

347
00:14:15,035 --> 00:14:17,510
and the need to write
reentrant code,

348
00:14:17,510 --> 00:14:19,580
you always have to
think about that.

349
00:14:19,580 --> 00:14:22,295
It's not a case where
you can say, well,

350
00:14:22,295 --> 00:14:23,330
I'm just going to execute

351
00:14:23,330 --> 00:14:24,785
this code from start to finish,

352
00:14:24,785 --> 00:14:26,135
I'm not gonna do any sleeps,

353
00:14:26,135 --> 00:14:28,175
so therefore I won't
be interrupted.

354
00:14:28,175 --> 00:14:30,125
That's never a good assumption

355
00:14:30,125 --> 00:14:31,820
and that's not something
that you can use.

356
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:34,685
You always need to think about
reentrancy and concurrency

357
00:14:34,685 --> 00:14:38,670
when you're doing kernel
development in general.